timestamp=2026-11-04T12:38:35.779Z level=INFO run=56dc2648 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Find temperature for a given zone and time - interpolate? Or take closest reading?\n# Readings are every 2 hours. Spoilage events are unmistakable trace when temps go way above safe.\n# The big batch was dispatched at 2023-07-18 03:17:00. Temp readings around then:\n# 02:00:00 Frozen was -3.13 (> -10) and Meat was 15.97 (> 4), 04:00:00 similar. So at dispatch time ~03:17, Frozen zone and Meat zone were above safe.\n# That means products from those zones dispatched at that time are likely spoiled. The question says \"Disposal write-offs are rare and, when they occur, leave an unmistakable trace in the data. Only exclude a dispatch from revenue if you are certain it represents a disposal event — when in doubt, count it as a sale.\"\n\n# \"Leave an unmistakable trace in the data\" - perhaps the temperature violation itself is the trace. Or maybe there's another indicator? Also look at the batch: multiple products in one dispatch batch (same timestamp). Some are Frozen category, some are Meat category.\n# Frozen category products (ice cream, frozen shrimp, frozen wings, frozen veg) stored in frozen zone - frozen zone went above -10 (to -3.13, -2.64). That’s a clear temperature abuse event -> likely spoilage -> disposal write-off. So that entire dispatch batch of frozen items from that timestamp (03:17:00) should NOT be counted as revenue.\n# Meat category products (salmon, chicken breast, ground beef) stored in Meat zone - temps soared to 15+ °C, way above 4°C. Also spoilage -> write-off. So meat items in that same batch also not counted.\n# That’s a massive spoilage event affecting all products dispatched at 03:17:00 on 2023-07-18 that belong to zones that exceeded max safe temp? Let’s verify.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
